# Trivy Ignore File — Suppress false-positive vulnerability findings
# Each entry must include the vulnerability ID and a justification.
# Review periodically (quarterly) to check if Trivy's DB has been updated.

# CVE-2026-1260 (GHSA-38vq-g6vr-w8wf) — sentencepiece heap overflow
# Affects: sentencepiece < 0.2.1
# Status: FIXED in sentencepiece==0.2.1 (which we pin in requirements.txt)
# The v0.2.1 release notes explicitly state "Security fix to address a heap overflow issue".
# Trivy's vulnerability database may not yet reflect that 0.2.1 is the patched version.
# This is a confirmed false positive — our pinned version IS the fix.
CVE-2026-1260

# KSV118 — Helm Tiller deployed (Trivy misidentifies `fmd-distiller` as Tiller)
# Reason: pod name "fmd-distiller" contains the string "tiller"; there is no Helm Tiller
# running in this cluster. Suppress on all pod spec templates.
KSV118

# CVE-2024-23342 (PYSEC-2026-1325) — python-ecdsa Minerva timing attack on P-256
# Affects: ecdsa (transitive via python-jose, hard-required — Requires-Dist: ecdsa!=0.15).
# Now explicit-pinned to ecdsa==0.19.2 in requirements.txt and 7 workers/* services
# (gateway-service, gbrain-bridge, infinity-admin-service, infinity-auth,
# infinity-one-service, infinity-portal-service, sentinel-station-service) — the pin
# itself fixes the separate PYSEC-2026-2467 DoS CVE, but made this manifest-visible
# to Trivy for the first time (previously unpinned/transitive, so not scanned as a
# direct dependency). No fixed version exists for this CVE specifically
# (fix_versions: [] in the advisory) — the ecdsa maintainers have stated
# side-channel attacks are out of scope. Every listed service's JWT usage is
# HS256/RS256-only (grep-confirmed no ES256/384/512 usage), so the vulnerable
# ECDSA sign/ECDH code paths are never invoked. Full context: SECURITY.md
# "Accepted Risks" table. Re-check trigger: ES256/ES384/ES512 JWT support added,
# or a patched ecdsa release ships for this CVE specifically.
CVE-2024-23342

# CVE-2025-69872 (GHSA-w8v5-vhqr-4h9v) — diskcache unsafe pickle deserialization
# Affects: diskcache <= 5.6.3 (workers/cache-service). 5.6.3 is the LATEST release
# and the advisory has NO fixed version, so there is no version to upgrade to.
# Status: NOT EXPLOITABLE in our usage. workers/cache-service/worker.py opens the
# cache with a hardened _NoPickleJSONDisk (JSONDisk subclass) that serializes/reads
# values as JSON AND hard-rejects the base Disk's pickle read paths (mode=MODE_PICKLE
# values and non-raw keys) — so a tampered or legacy pickle row raises (cache miss)
# instead of reaching pickle.load(). Verified with an adversarial MODE_PICKLE
# injection test. The finding remains only because scanners match the package
# version (usage-blind), not the serializer in use. Re-evaluate (and drop this
# ignore) if a patched diskcache release ships. Full context at the pin in
# workers/cache-service/requirements-worker.txt.
CVE-2025-69872
